.TH NEWFILE 1 "August 2026" "newfile 0.1.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
newfile \- create files with specified attributes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B newfile
[\fB\-Abfpv\fR] [\fB\-m\fR \fImode\fR] [\fB\-o\fR \fIowner\fR[:\fIgroup\fR]]
[\fB\-s\fR \fIsize\fR]
.br
.in +8n
[\fB\-\-reference\fR=\fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-sparse\fR]
[\fB\-\-template\fR=\fIfile\fR] \fIfile\fR ...
.in
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B newfile
creates each
.I file
with the mode, ownership, size and initial content given on the command
line, in a single invocation.
.PP
Each
.I file
is opened with owner read and write only, filled, given its owner, then
given the requested mode, so it is never readable under the wrong
ownership.  A file that cannot be completed is not created: if any step
fails, or the run is interrupted by a signal, the partial file is
removed.
.PP
By default an existing
.I file
is not replaced.  The underlying
.BR open (2)
call uses
.BR O_EXCL ,
so the existence check and the creation are atomic: if two processes
race to create the same path, only one succeeds.  Pass
.B \-f
to replace an existing file.  The replacement is committed with
.BR rename (2),
which is atomic in the same way.
.PP
An operand ending in a slash names a directory and is rejected, because
.B newfile
creates only regular files.  Paths are otherwise handed to the system
as typed, so a
.B ..
component follows a symbolic link rather than cancelling the name in
front of it.
.PP
If
.B \-m
is not given, the mode defaults to
.BR a=rw ,
subject to the process umask.  Pass
.B \-A
to apply the requested bits exactly, ignoring the umask.
.PP
Long options must be spelled in full.  Options may appear before or
after the file operands.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-mode\fR=\fImode\fR
Set the file permission bits.
.I mode
may be octal (e.g.\& \fB0755\fR) or symbolic (e.g.\&
\fBu=rwx,g=rx,o=r\fR).  The symbolic syntax is
\fB[ugoa...][=+-][rwxst...][,...]\fR, a subset of the
.BR chmod (1)
syntax.  Each clause takes a single operator.  The
.BR chmod (1)
permission letters
.BR X ,
.BR u ,
.BR g ,
and
.B o
are not accepted.  A clause without a who letter applies to all
users, as if
.B a
were given.
.TP
\fB\-\-reference\fR=\fIfile\fR
Copy the mode of
.I file
rather than spelling it out with
.BR \-m .
The copied mode is subject to the umask, like a mode given with
.BR \-m .
Combine with
.B \-A
to copy the mode exactly.
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-m .
.TP
\fB\-A\fR, \fB\-\-absolute\fR
Apply the mode supplied with
.B \-m
(or the default mode) exactly, without clearing bits through the
umask.  Only the file mode is affected.  Parent directories created
with
.B \-p
remain subject to the umask.
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-owner\fR=\fIowner\fR[:\fIgroup\fR]
Set the file owner, and optionally the group, of each new file, using
the ordering described above.  Any set-user-ID or set-group-ID bits
requested with
.B \-m
survive, because the mode is applied after the ownership and
.BR chown (2)
clears them.
.I owner
and
.I group
may each be a name or a numeric ID.  A colon must be followed by a
group, so
.B \-o
.I owner:
is rejected rather than treated as
.B \-o
.IR owner .
Changing ownership generally requires appropriate privileges.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-size\fR=\fIsize\fR
Fill the new file with NUL bytes to the given
.IR size .
Suffixes
.BR K ,
.BR M ,
and
.B G
are supported, using powers of 1024.
Use
.B \-\-sparse
to create a sparse file instead of writing NUL bytes.
.TP
.B \-\-sparse
With
.BR \-\-size ,
create a sparse file rather than filling it with NUL bytes.
Unwritten regions consume no disk blocks on filesystems that support
sparse files.
Without
.BR \-\-size ,
this option has no effect and a warning is printed.
.TP
\fB\-\-template\fR=\fIfile\fR
Copy the contents of
.I file
into each new file.  The resulting size is the size of the template.
If
.I file
is
.BR \- ,
the content is read from standard input.  When several files are
given, the input is read once and written to every one.
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-s .
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-parents\fR
Create any missing parent directories along the target path, similar
to
.BR "mkdir \-p" .
New directories receive mode
.B 0777
subject to the umask.
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-force\fR
Replace
.I file
if it already exists.  The replacement is built beside the target and
moved onto it with
.BR rename (2),
so a reader gets either the old file or the new one.  The replacement
is flushed with
.BR fsync (2)
before the rename and its directory after, so a crash during the
operation also leaves one file or the other.  It carries the requested
attributes exactly as a new file would.  If
.B \-m
is not given its mode is set to the default
.BR a=rw .
.IP
Because the replacement is a different file, the path takes a new
inode.  Other hard links to the old file go on naming the old content,
and where
.I file
is a symbolic link it is the link that is replaced rather than its
target.  Replacing also needs write permission on the directory holding
.IR file ,
which rewriting in place would not.
.TP
\fB\-\-backup\fR[\fB=\fIcontrol\fR]
Before replacing with
.BR \-f ,
give the existing file a second name.  Without
.BR \-f ,
this option has no effect and a warning is printed.
The backup is a hard link, so it holds the original inode and
.I file
itself never stops existing.  A backup already present under the
chosen name is replaced.
Only a regular file is backed up.
.B newfile
reports an error for a directory or symbolic link rather than
linking it.
.IP
.I control
selects the backup scheme:
.RS
.TP
.B none\fR (alias \fBoff\fR)
Make no backup, even when
.B \-\-backup
is given.
.TP
.B numbered\fR (alias \fBt\fR)
Append
\fB.~\fIn\fR\fB~\fR,
choosing the next available number.
.TP
.B existing\fR (alias \fBnil\fR)
Use numbered when a numbered backup already exists, otherwise simple.
.TP
.B simple\fR (alias \fBnever\fR)
Append
.B ~
to the file name.
.RE
.IP
If
.I control
is omitted, the value of the
.B VERSION_CONTROL
environment variable is consulted.  If that is also unset,
.B existing
is used.
.TP
.B \-b
Equivalent to
.B \-\-backup
with no
.I control
argument.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Print a short message for each file as it is created.  The message
names the backup when one is made.
.TP
\fB\-\-help\fR
Display a summary of options and exit.
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
Print version information and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All files were processed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred for one or more files, or an unknown option was
supplied.  A per-file failure does not stop processing of the remaining
operands.
.IP
Exceeding the process file size limit is reported as an ordinary
per-file error rather than terminating the run.
.IP
One error is reported after the file is already in place: a failed
directory flush under
.BR \-f .
The replacement is complete and correct, but the rename may not survive
a crash.  A directory that cannot be opened for reading, and a
filesystem that does not flush directories at all, are not errors.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VERSION_CONTROL
When
.B \-b
or
.B \-\-backup
is given without an explicit
.IR control ,
this variable selects the backup scheme.  Its recognized values are
the same as the
.I control
argument to
.BR \-\-backup .
If unset,
.B existing
is used.
.TP
.B COLUMNS
Width in columns for the
.B \-\-help
output.  A value below 40 or above 1000, or one that is not a decimal
number, is ignored and 80 is used.  A word too long for the remaining
space is left to overrun rather than broken.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create an executable shell script:
.PP
.RS
.nf
newfile \-m 0755 script.sh
.fi
.RE
.PP
Apply exact symbolic permissions, ignoring the umask:
.PP
.RS
.nf
newfile \-A \-m u=rw,g=r,o=r config.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Drop a private key into a directory that may not yet exist:
.PP
.RS
.nf
newfile \-p \-m 0600 \-o www\-data:www\-data /var/www/secrets/app.key
.fi
.RE
.PP
Allocate a 10 MiB sparse file (no disk blocks consumed):
.PP
.RS
.nf
newfile \-m 0600 \-s 10M \-\-sparse /tmp/swapfile
.fi
.RE
.PP
Copy the mode of an existing file onto a new one:
.PP
.RS
.nf
newfile \-\-reference=existing.conf new.conf
.fi
.RE
.PP
Seed a new file from a template:
.PP
.RS
.nf
newfile \-\-template=skeleton.conf \-m 0600 app.conf
.fi
.RE
.PP
Replace a file while keeping a numbered backup of the previous
version:
.PP
.RS
.nf
newfile \-f \-\-backup=numbered \-m 0644 config.txt
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR chmod (1),
.BR chown (1),
.BR cp (1),
.BR install (1),
.BR touch (1),
.BR truncate (1),
.BR open (2),
.BR link (2),
.BR rename (2),
.BR fsync (2),
